event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0a67329957b78268006a74dafb14c88196a75decb663f338e8","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":291,"text":"The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA says the rover captured a first-of-its-kind observation: Earth disappearing behind Mars’ moon Phobos as seen from the surface of another planet. The image sequence was taken by Perseverance’s Mastcam-Z camera on July 2, 2026, during the rover’s 1,907th Martian day, and NASA/JPL published the story on Aug. 5, 2026【5:1†source】.\n\nKey details:\n\n- Earth appears as a tiny bright dot in the Martian sky while Phobos crosses in front of it, briefly occulting it【5:1†source】.\n- NASA described it as a “unique Earth self-portrait” from Mars, with a “Phobos photobomb”【5:1†source】.\n- At the time, Earth was about 195 million miles — 314 million kilometers — away from Perseverance, making it appear as a single pixel-size dot【5:1†source】.\n- Perseverance is observing from the rim of Jezero Crater, where it continues its science campaign【5:1†source】.\n\nA recent science update from July also reported that Perseverance studied a rock unit called the “Broom Point member” on Jezero Crater’s rim. NASA said the 245-foot-thick, or 75-meter-thick, stack of ancient rock appears to record repeated asteroid impacts from early Mars history【5:2†source】."}

